<!-- Avatar sprite: every drawn part of the generated student chickadee, once.

     The second sprite in the codebase, and deliberately not part of the icon
     one: icons are stroked 24x24 Feather glyphs inlined on every page, while
     these are flat-filled 64x64 plumage parts wanted only by the pages that
     show an avatar. Include this partial from those pages, ONCE per page; a
     call site is one svg carrying class "avatar" wrapping six use elements,
     each with an href that is a same-page fragment naming a symbol below. Copy
     a live call site rather than working from this description.

     SEVEN AXES VARY, and nothing else: cap family, wing pattern, expression,
     accessory, backdrop, tuft and tilt. Body, cheek, beak and bib are fixed -
     they are what keeps every bird a chickadee even when the cap goes plum.
     Layer order back to front: backdrop, tuft, plumage, wings, expression,
     accessory. The tuft sits BEFORE the plumage so the body circle covers its
     base, and a hat drawn later covers the tuft on purpose. Tilt is not a
     symbol: it is one rotate transform on a group that wraps every layer but
     the backdrop.

     An expression may redraw a fixed part over the plumage - the open beak
     draws a mouth and a split beak on top of the closed one - and the brow
     and closed-eye strokes are pale on the cap, because a cap-coloured stroke
     on the cap is invisible.

     The bird is symmetrical and front-facing: each wing symbol draws one wing
     and then the same content again inside a mirroring group, so both wings
     come from one drawing. The mirror is a transform rather than a second set
     of coordinates because an arc cannot be reflected by negating its x values
     alone - its sweep flag has to invert too, and getting that wrong yields a
     subtly, silently wrong curve.

     THREE RULES THIS FILE MUST KEEP.

     1. No clip-path, mask, filter or gradient. A clipPath referenced from a
        symbol inside a display:none sprite silently does not apply in Chromium
        - the shape renders UNCLIPPED, so a cap drawn as a rectangle and
        trimmed to the head becomes a rectangle across the whole viewBox. It
        looks like a rendering fault and it is invisible to any test that only
        asserts the page renders. Every shape here is instead a closed path
        that already follows the body arc, which is also why a wing is a path
        rather than an ellipse: its outer edge IS the body circle. A transform
        is fine - unlike a url() reference, it resolves no ids.

     2. Colour comes from a class, never an attribute. Each part carries one of
        the av-* classes, which resolve to fill: var(...) in styles.css. The
        four varying ones read per-avatar custom properties; the fixed ones
        read the palette directly. A literal fill here would be a raw colour
        outside the palette and would not follow dark mode.

     3. The body is a circle of radius 21 centred at (32, 34) in a 64x64
        viewBox, and the two eye discs are radius 7.6 at y 29.5, 7.8 either
        side of centre. Every boundary is computed against those, so changing
        one means recomputing the cap, wing and lid edges rather than nudging
        them by eye. Tools/avatar-preview renders a contact sheet.

     Described rather than quoted, like the icon sprite: a fragment reference
     written in prose is indistinguishable from a real one to any scanner
     reading this file. -->
<svg class="icon-sprite" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <defs>
        <symbol id="av-backdrop" viewBox="0 0 64 64">
